# Files that must keep LF endings regardless of the platform they are edited on.
#
# Scoped deliberately rather than using `* text=auto`. A blanket rule would
# renormalise every tracked file on the next commit, which buries a real change
# in a thousand-line whitespace diff. These are the files where CRLF is not
# cosmetic:
#
#   Dockerfile  -- built on Linux by the host. A CRLF line ending becomes part
#                  of the command, so `RUN foo` runs `foo\r` and fails with a
#                  "not found" that names a command that looks correct.
#   *.sh        -- same problem, worse error: the shebang becomes `/bin/sh\r`.
#   *.yml/*.yaml -- CI and host config files, read on Linux.
#
# Editing the Dockerfile on Windows is what introduced the CRLF churn this rule
# now prevents.

Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
